Invited Talks & Keynotes
Invited presentations provide an opportunity to discuss research results, emerging technologies, and broader developments in information security and digital trust.
Topics have included:

information security and cryptography;
digital signatures and public key infrastructure;
digital identity;
electronic documents and digital preservation;
blockchain and cryptocurrencies;
post-quantum cryptography;
artificial intelligence and security;
trustworthy digital infrastructures.

Public slides, videos, proceedings, or event pages will be linked whenever they are available.

Conference Presentations
Presentations associated with scientific publications will progressively be connected to their corresponding bibliographic records.
A presentation record may include:

event;
presentation title;
date and location;
authors or presenters;
associated publication;
slides;
video;
event program;
persistent archival copy.

Lecture Notes & Textbooks
Teaching materials developed for undergraduate and graduate courses form an important part of this collection.
Topics include:

mathematical foundations for computer science;
information security;
cryptography;
digital signatures;
public key infrastructure;
digital identity;
electronic documents;
blockchain and cryptocurrencies;
artificial intelligence and security;
security and usability in information systems.

Some of these materials originated as course notes and evolved over several years of teaching. Where appropriate, they will be reorganized and preserved as independent academic texts.

Technical Texts
This collection will also include substantial technical material produced outside the conventional scientific-publication workflow.
Examples include:

technical reports;
technology studies;
architecture documents;
security analyses;
cryptographic infrastructure studies;
standards-related material;
implementation guides;
institutional technical documents.

When possible, public versions will be preserved together with their original publication context and associated projects.

Preservation and Versions
Long-form academic material frequently exists in several forms: drafts, course editions, institutional reports, preprints, published versions, and revised editions.
The archive will therefore distinguish between a work and its individual versions.
Whenever appropriate, preserved works may include:

bibliographic metadata;
publication date;
version history;
PDF;
source files;
DOI or other persistent identifier;
associated software or datasets;
related publications;
related courses;
related projects.

The objective is to preserve both the intellectual work and enough context to understand its evolution.

Emerging Directions

Post-Quantum Cryptography
The transition to post-quantum cryptography creates new challenges for digital signatures, PKI, network protocols and long-lived digital documents.
My research investigates post-quantum cryptographic mechanisms and their integration into existing security infrastructures.
Particular interests include post-quantum signatures, hybrid cryptographic systems, migration strategies and crypto-agility.


Artificial Intelligence & Security
Artificial intelligence is creating a new class of actors in digital systems.
My current research investigates the security and trust requirements of AI systems and autonomous agents, particularly questions involving:

agent identity;
authentication;
authorization;
delegation;
accountability;
auditability;
non-repudiation.

An important direction is the concept of Personal AI Agents: autonomous agents acting on behalf of individuals and interacting with organizations, services and other agents.

My work is centered on the security and trust mechanisms required for digital systems.
My main research interests include:

information security;
cryptography and cryptographic protocols;
digital signatures and electronic signatures;
public key infrastructure (PKI);
digital identity and authentication;
electronic documents and long-term preservation;
cryptographic key management;
arithmetic over finite fields;
post-quantum cryptography;
artificial intelligence applied to security and digital trust.

A recurring theme in my research is the connection between cryptographic foundations and deployable systems: how mathematical, cryptographic and security mechanisms can be transformed into infrastructures and applications that operate in real institutional environments.

Research Collaboration
I welcome academic and technical collaboration in areas related to:

information security;
cryptography;
digital signatures;
public key infrastructure;
digital identity;
electronic documents;
finite-field arithmetic;
post-quantum cryptography;
artificial intelligence and security;
digital trust infrastructures.
